From ff1d55b99f55cb7dfe7e74f58c7228e17d7bddfc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ms178 <ms178@example.com>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 16:38:35 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] ORACLE-CACHE-1: one persistent Compiler Explorer cache
 for the whole tree

Four tools ask Compiler Explorer for the same (compiler, flags, source)
tuples and each had grown its own memoisation. Two of the three designs were
individually reasonable; the defect was that there were three of them.

The sharp end: the execution oracle from PR #677 is the heaviest CE consumer
in the tree by construction -- the API returns assembly only from a
NON-executing request, so it issues two requests per compiler per program --
and it memoised in a process-wide dict. Every sweep re-bought ~48 requests,
could not be resumed after a 429, could not reproduce a table it had already
published once the process exited, and could not run in CI at all. That is
the same failure scripts/oracle_asm.py was written to work around.

scripts/encdiff.py had a second, independent bug: its cache defaulted to the
RELATIVE path ".godbolt-cache", so where it cached depended on the working
directory it was invoked from. Run it from anywhere but the repo root and it
built a private cache there, re-issuing everything and scattering
.godbolt-cache/ over the filesystem.

scripts/godbolt_cache.py is now the single implementation, used by all four:

  * NAMESPACED (att-v2 / oracle-v1 / encdiff-v1). The namespace is a format
    contract, not a label: att-v2 exists because a pre-v2 cache stored Intel
    syntax and made AT&T load/store metrics read as zero in a table that
    looked like a triumph. Bumping it is how a format change invalidates old
    records instead of poisoning new ones.
  * COLLISION-PROOF: parts are joined with NUL, so no source text can forge a
    field boundary. ("a,b","c") and ("a","b,c") must not collide -- in a
    compiler oracle a forged key serves one program's result for another's.
  * ATOMIC per (pid, thread): --rank fans out over threads, and a killed
    sweep must never leave a truncated record that reads as a hit.
  * CORRUPTION IS A MISS, never an exception.
  * EMPTY IS A HIT: an inlined-away body is real data, not a miss.

remote() no longer caches every failure. Caching a transport failure
persistently would turn one transient 429 into a permanent verdict: the
oracle would be recorded as failed, skipped by every future sweep, and
never come back without deleting the cache by hand -- a silently shrinking
oracle set that still reports PASS on the oracles that did run. Transport
errors are retried next run; deterministic ones (the oracle rejects the
program, CE declines to execute) are cached.

Adds --no-cache and --cache-stats. scripts/test_godbolt_cache.py pins all of
the above offline in 13 checks (namespace isolation, separator forging,
truncated records, 64 concurrent writers, empty bodies, unicode source) and
is gated in ci_local.sh and GitHub CI. Upstream's own 11-test oracle
selftest still passes unmodified -- the change is transport-level.
